# ENG-2478: Add a type filter panel to the Obsidian node summoning menu

## Context

ENG-893 builds the Obsidian node summoning menu, a popover at the cursor that groups
discourse nodes by type, up to 10 per type. It has no type filter. So Obsidian is behind
Roam, and in a large vault the type a user wants can be pushed out of its 10 slots.

This ticket adds a header row with a filter button, and the filter panel opens inside the menu.
The branch is stacked on `eng-893-implement-node-summoning-menu-in-obsidian`. The PR targets
that branch, and ENG-893 must merge first.

**Decisions made (2026-11-13):**
- Restructure `NodeTypeFilterMenu` so its panel can render inline. Advanced search keeps the
  dropdown form, and the summon menu renders the panel inline.
- Use **"Clear filter (N)"**, as advanced search does, instead of Roam's "Select all" /
  "All selected".
- **Unchecking every type shows all types.** This keeps `discourseNodeTypeFilter.ts` as it is:
  an empty selection means no filter.
- The ticket's Done When is rewritten to match (step 1).

## Step 1: Rewrite the ticket (after approval)

Update ENG-2478's Solution and Done When in Linear. Use `dg-engineering-writing-style`, then
read the ticket back to check it. The ticket stays in `Ticket Draft` until you move it.

**Solution (replace the reuse bullets):**
- Split `NodeTypeFilterMenu` so its panel can render inline. Advanced search keeps the
  dropdown, and the summon menu renders the panel inside the menu.
- Pass the selected types to `rankDiscourseNodesByTitle` through `nodeTypeIds`.

**Done When:**
- [ ] The menu header has a filter button. Clicking it opens the panel inside the menu without
  closing the menu or moving focus out of the editor.
- [ ] All types start checked. Unchecking a type removes its group from the results.
- [ ] "Only" selects just that type and collapses the panel (ENG-1046).
- [ ] When a filter is active, the panel shows "Clear filter (N)". Clicking it checks every
  type again, and the filter button shows as active.
- [ ] Unchecking the last checked type returns to all types, as in advanced search.
- [ ] The filter resets to all types each time the menu opens.
- [ ] Typing after the trigger still narrows results inside the filtered types.
- [ ] Advanced search's type filter looks and works as before.

**Notes (add):** The menu uses advanced search's "Clear filter (N)" instead of Roam's
"Select all", so the two Obsidian filters match.

## Step 2: Split `NodeTypeFilterMenu` (slice 1, advanced search unchanged)

File: `apps/obsidian/src/components/NodeTypeFilterMenu.tsx`

- Export `NodeTypeFilterPanel` and move the canonical-to-panel conversion into it.
  `hasActiveTypeFilter`, `toPanelSelectedIds` and `fromPanelSelectedIds` from
  `utils/discourseNodeTypeFilter.ts` are reused as they are. Both hosts then pass canonical ids,
  where `[]` means all.
  - Props: `nodeTypes`, `selectedNodeTypeIds`, `onSelectedNodeTypeIdsChange`,
    `isInline?: boolean`, `onSelectOnly?: () => void`.
  - `isInline` hides the type search box and uses a shorter list cap (about `max-h-40`).
    The search box needs DOM focus, which the menu blocks, and it would take focus from the
    editor. Roam has no type search either.
  - `onSelectOnly` runs after "Only" changes the selection, so the summon menu can collapse
    the panel. Advanced search doesn't pass it, so "Only" works there as before.
- `NodeTypeFilterMenu` keeps its own props and becomes `SearchDropdown` wrapped around
  `<NodeTypeFilterPanel />`. It still computes `isFilterActive` for the badge and aria label.
- The Clear filter button's focus parking (`closest("[data-search-dropdown-panel]")?.focus()`)
  already handles a missing element, so it is safe inline.

Callers (shared-component rule): `NodeTypeFilterMenu` is used only by `NodeSearchModal`.
`NodeTypeFilterPanel` is used by that and the new summon-menu caller. Take a before and after
screenshot of advanced search's filter dropdown.

→ **Pause for your manual check of advanced search.**

## Step 3: Wire the filter into the summon menu (slice 2)

**`apps/obsidian/src/utils/nodeSummonResults.ts`:** add `nodeTypeIds?: string[]` to
`groupNodesForSummonMenu` and pass it through to `rankDiscourseNodesByTitle`, which already
filters before scoring, with `[]` and `undefined` both meaning all.

**`apps/obsidian/src/components/NodeSummonMenu.tsx`:**
- New class fields `selectedNodeTypeIds: string[] = []` and `isFilterOpen = false`. Each open
  builds a new instance, which handles "resets each open" with no extra code.
- `regroup()` passes `nodeTypeIds: this.selectedNodeTypeIds`. On a filter change: regroup, set
  `activeIndex = 0`, then render. This mirrors Roam snapping the highlight to the first row.
- `render()` mounts a small wrapper. It renders the header row (filter button), then
  `NodeTypeFilterPanel isInline` when `isFilterOpen`, then the existing list. The header and
  panel are siblings above `.suggestion`, so they stay pinned while the results scroll. The
  header also renders in the empty state, so the user can widen the filter when nothing shows.
- Filter button: a `clickable-icon` with Obsidian's `filter` icon (`setIcon`, as
  `SearchDropdown` does) and the aria label "Filter by type". It gets `is-active` when the panel
  is open or a filter is active.
- `onSelectOnly` sets `isFilterOpen = false`.
- No changes are needed to keyboard, focus or outside-click handling:
  - The container's blanket `mousedown` `preventDefault` keeps the editor focused for every
    panel click.
  - The outside-click guard checks `containerEl.contains(...)`, which covers the inline panel.
  - The keymap `Scope` keeps the arrow keys, Enter and Escape on the results. Escape closes
    the whole menu, as in Roam.
- The `ResizeObserver` already reruns placement when the panel grows. Opening it near the
  bottom of a pane can flip the menu upward, which is expected.

→ **Pause for your manual check of the summon menu.**

